---
title: Chicken Spinach and Vegetable Stir-Fry
servings: 4
time: 30 min
tags: chicken, stir-fry, quick, main
course: main
---

Thaw the @chicken strips{450%g} safely in the refrigerator, then pat dry. If the strips are long, cut them into bite-sized pieces. Whisk together the @soy sauce{2%tbsp}, @water{2%tbsp}, @cornflour{1%tbsp}, @rice wine vinegar{2%tbsp}, @tomato sauce{2%tbsp}, and @brown sugar{1 1/2%tbsp} in a small bowl.

Heat half the @neutral oil{2%tbsp} in a #large wok{} or #large frying pan{} over high heat. For raw chicken strips, cook in a single layer, in batches if needed, until browned and cooked through, about 4–6 minutes per batch. Check the thickest piece is no longer pink inside, then transfer to a plate. For precooked strips, skip this step and add them later with the spinach, just long enough to heat through.

Add the remaining oil to the pan. Stir-fry the @frozen mixed vegetables{300%g} until hot and most of their released moisture has evaporated, about 4–6 minutes. Add the @capsicum{1}(sliced) and cook for 2 minutes.

Add the @garlic{2%cloves}(minced) and @ginger{1%tsp}(grated or minced); stir for 30 seconds. Add the @tomato{1}(cut into wedges) and cook for 1–2 minutes, just until it starts to soften.

If using raw chicken, return it and any juices to the pan. Stir the sauce again, pour it in, and toss until it bubbles and lightly thickens, about 1–2 minutes. If using precooked chicken, pour in the sauce and stir until glossy and lightly thickened, about 1–2 minutes, then add the chicken. Add the @spinach{100%g}(roughly chopped) and toss just until wilted and the precooked chicken is hot. Taste and adjust with @black pepper{} or a little more soy sauce if needed.

Serve hot with @cooked rice{} or @cooked noodles{}.

> Swaps to consider: Add @?pineapple juice{2%tbsp} in place of the water for extra fruitiness, @?sesame oil{1%tsp} at the end for a toasted aroma, @?chilli flakes{1/2%tsp} for heat, or @?spring onions{2}(sliced) as a fresh garnish. Frozen chicken strips must be thawed before stir-frying; thawing time is not included in the recipe time.

> Source blend: Hill Street Grocer's Quick Chicken Stir Fry (https://hillstreetgrocer.com/recipes/main-course-recipes/quick-chicken-stir-fry) supplied the chicken, capsicum, tomato and spinach combination and finish-with-spinach sequence. The US Department of Veterans Affairs Chicken and Vegetable Stir-Fry (https://www.nutrition.va.gov/NUTRITION/docs/EESDocuments/ChickenAndVegetableStirFry.pdf) informed the use of vinegar and a cornflour-thickened sauce. The sweet-sour balance here adapts BBC Food's Sweet and Sour Chicken (https://bbc.com.im/food/recipes/sweet_and_sour_chicken_00397), using vinegar, tomato sauce, sugar and soy. Metro South Health's Chicken Stir Fry (https://www.metrosouth.health.qld.gov.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0022/242194/Chicken-stir-fry.pdf) supports using frozen stir-fry vegetables and cooking chicken separately. Quantities and the precooked-strip adaptation are estimates; timing is untested.
